#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e012e206-3735-52c5-a282-bf2808614912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339014} {:answer/id #uuid "856326c3-7667-54fa-a934-161221b007ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339015} {:answer/id #uuid "e800c784-13ae-5601-9c36-93a4a0f3cd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339016} {:answer/id #uuid "fc573f65-a24e-520c-8eed-2b957f1759f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339017} {:answer/id #uuid "d421e68e-e389-5822-acdd-369907b97f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e800c784-13ae-5601-9c36-93a4a0f3cd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339016} :answer-field/id #uuid "48eb6605-a5a6-5deb-9bd1-c52985f6ec88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27bea004-4b63-5df6-b1f8-256ce7d09483" :question/math-academy-id "q-15759" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡t & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4c7c790-d016-564e-8f2a-a689ca3b0dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302869} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9325d7-f550-5c59-88d7-8ff8a9233ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302870} {:answer/id #uuid "b87282ce-139d-532c-be53-6935258cb3e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-18+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302871} {:answer/id #uuid "b806830b-01c1-5815-b667-6de6e14cfc1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302872} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce38dcf-0d4f-57ad-abc3-15b67f37213c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4c7c790-d016-564e-8f2a-a689ca3b0dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302869} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c96509-f5e7-56e3-8067-f632bbdd7285" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f6300777-605f-507f-978f-04a8f9538fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-24576" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e5208b2-72e0-53de-b598-4f8f6bd822e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e5208b2-72e0-53de-b598-4f8f6bd822e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021} :answer-field/id #uuid "394f49e8-fe69-5db4-b535-7ab3013652df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "992d1ab3-8ed0-513c-9be8-a224e42f96bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-276277" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}+4,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=t+1\\,⇒\\,t=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x-1)^{2}+4 \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y={x}^{2}-2x+5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be260f14-3c7a-5677-885c-0241dadd515e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339024} {:answer/id #uuid "85c5029b-6042-5acf-b9c3-c3eef283ec26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be260f14-3c7a-5677-885c-0241dadd515e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339024} :answer-field/id #uuid "b71634b4-798f-5272-8da0-3e322ce25d66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a6c0c76-c6e1-50d8-a750-17e2c1877457" :question/math-academy-id "q-321090" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9},\\,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,t\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for ${t}^{2}:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}\\,⇒\\,{t}^{2}={x}^{2}+9\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${t}^{2}={x}^{2}+9$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=3({x}^{2}+9)=3{x}^{2}+27.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3{x}^{2}+27.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 3$ and $x=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is $y=3{x}^{2}+27,\\,x\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021c6d5c-2559-569e-a7f5-d9c0a624fa15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x^2}{18}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "021c6d5c-2559-569e-a7f5-d9c0a624fa15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x^2}{18}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339028} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbb48a5f-dd43-5754-a144-f2da4c59a204" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8042d0b-4861-581a-af53-dc262d902695" :question/math-academy-id "q-321318" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36})-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{36}-1 \\\\ y & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5.$"}]}
